  "what_each_side_is_given": {
   "openfoam": "chemFoam as the solver agent of case 20 ran it: one cell on one core of the Ryzen 7 7800X3D, the chemistry solver seulex (implicit) with relative tolerance 1e-8 and absolute tolerance 1e-16 kmol/m3, 2500 to 80000 equal steps, a log line and two output rows written at every step. Its times are read from results/h2_air_ignition_box/runs/ and are not rerun. Its looser and its explicit runs (RKF45; tolerance 0.1) are quoted next to it. OpenFOAM's constants.\n",
   "gpucfd": "The kernel on the RTX 4070. For the one-page report: the same single parcel in the same numbers of steps, double precision with tolerance 1e-8 (OpenFOAM's), where the GPU has one cell to work on and the time is all launch overhead. For the throughput: batches of cells in the chosen precision and tolerance. CHEMKIN's constants for the main answer; OpenFOAM's for the direct comparison of numbers.\n",
   "limits_to_state": "One cell on one core against a batch on a GPU is not like for like: OpenFOAM's 100 microseconds per step include its per-step overhead for a single cell and its output, and a flow solver would spread cells over 8 cores. OpenFOAM was not tuned for speed. Tolerances mean different things in the two codes. gpucfd's batch numbers are for cells that all do the same work, and for a mixed population measured separately.\n"
  }
